      {"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"TechArticle","@id":"https://lab.colormeup.co/apca-vs-wcag#article","headline":"APCA vs WCAG","description":"WCAG 2 and APCA pick different text colors for a wide band of mid-tone backgrounds. Both picks, side by side.","keywords":["APCA","WCAG","text contrast","color contrast","OKLCH"],"about":{"@id":"https://lab.colormeup.co/#webapp"},"image":"https://lab.colormeup.co/og-apca-vs-wcag.png","mainEntityOfPage":"https://lab.colormeup.co/apca-vs-wcag","url":"https://lab.colormeup.co/apca-vs-wcag","author":{"@id":"https://lab.colormeup.co/#creator"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://lab.colormeup.co/#org"},"isPartOf":{"@id":"https://lab.colormeup.co/#website"},"inLanguage":"en","datePublished":"2026-10-24","dateModified":"2026-10-24"},{"@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://lab.colormeup.co/apca-vs-wcag#faq","isPartOf":{"@id":"https://lab.colormeup.co/apca-vs-wcag#article"},"mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Which text color should go on my button?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On light and dark backgrounds, WCAG and APCA give the same answer, so use it. On mid-tones, from about OKLCH lightness 0.55 to 0.75 depending on hue and chroma, they split: WCAG picks black and APCA picks white. Put both versions side by side, as on this page, and use the one that reads better at your button’s size and weight."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does Lc mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lc is APCA’s lightness contrast score. It runs from 0, no contrast, to about 106 for black text on white and 108 for white text on black. Higher reads easier. It isn’t a ratio of two light levels like the WCAG number; it models how strong text looks against its background."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why can Lc be negative?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The sign is the polarity. Positive means dark text on a lighter background; negative means light text on a darker one. The size of the number is the